##:
net/tcp -- TcpStream and TcpListener over one file descriptor.

The module ships as bundled .sushi source and is merged as a compilation unit
when imported (`use <net/tcp>`). It composes the <net/socket> primitives; a
struct here is a typed name for a descriptor and carries nothing else.

A socket OWNS its descriptor. TcpStream and TcpListener implement the Drop
perk, so each one moves to exactly one owner and closes itself when that owner
leaves scope. There is nothing to remember and nothing to leak: the rule that
one binding owns a socket is enforced by the compiler rather than written down
here, and handing a stream to a `nom` parameter is what transfers it.

close() stays, for the caller who has to SEE that the close failed -- a
destructor cannot answer a Result. It consumes the handle, so the descriptor
is released exactly once and a use after the close is refused (CE2435).

Limitations: blocking sockets only, so connect has no timeout of its own
and an unreachable address waits for the kernel to give up. read_bytes() answers
whatever one read gave, which may be less than the maximum asked for;
recv_exact() is the loop for a caller that needs a count.
:##

use <collections/strings>
public use <io/contracts>
public use <net/error>
public use <net/handle>
public use <net/ip>
use <net/socket>

##:
One connected TCP stream.

It owns its descriptor: the stream moves to one owner and closes when that
owner leaves scope. Call close() when the failure has to be seen.
:##
public struct TcpStream:
    ##: The descriptor, or -1 once close() has run. :##
    i32 fd

##:
One listening TCP socket.

It owns its descriptor, on the same terms as TcpStream.
:##
public struct TcpListener:
    ##: The descriptor, or -1 once close() has run. :##
    i32 fd

##:
Closes the stream when its owner leaves scope.
:##
extend TcpStream with Drop:
    ##:
    Closes the descriptor.

    A destructor has nowhere to put a Result, so a close that fails here is lost.
    Call close() to see it.
    :##
    fn drop(poke self) ~:
        close_socket(poke self.fd)

##:
Closes the listener when its owner leaves scope. The same terms as TcpStream.
:##
extend TcpListener with Drop:
    ##:
    Closes the descriptor, on the same terms as the TcpStream destructor.
    :##
    fn drop(poke self) ~:
        close_socket(poke self.fd)

##:
Reads bytes from the stream. The same contract a File satisfies, so one generic serves
both.
:##
extend TcpStream with Reader:
    ##:
    Reads what one read gives, as text.

    The bound counts BYTES, so a multi-byte character can be split across two calls -- and
    on a stream it can be split by the NETWORK as well, whatever the bound says. A caller
    that must not cut one reads bytes, accumulates them, and converts once.

    - Parameter max: The most bytes to take in this one read.
    - Returns: The text that arrived, empty when the peer closed.
    - Errors: IoError, when the read failed.
    :##
    fn read(poke self, i32 max) string | IoError:
        let u8[] chunk = self.read_bytes(max)??
        return Result.Ok(chunk.to_string())

    ##:
    Reads what one read gives, as bytes.

    An empty answer means the peer closed cleanly. A timeout is an error rather than an
    empty answer, so the two never read alike.

    - Parameter max: The most bytes to take in this one read.
    - Returns: The bytes that arrived, empty when the peer closed.
    - Errors: IoError, when the read failed.
    :##
    fn read_bytes(poke self, i32 max) u8[] | IoError:
        match sock_recv(self.fd, max):
            Result.Ok(nom chunk) -> return Result.Ok(chunk)
            Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e.to_io())

##:
Writes bytes to the stream, and flushes what an unbuffered socket has no need to flush.
:##
extend TcpStream with Writer:
    ##:
    Writes text, and does not stop until every byte has gone.

    - Parameter data: The text to write.
    - Returns: Nothing.
    - Errors: IoError, when a write failed before the text was gone.
    :##
    fn write(poke self, string data) ~ | IoError:
        # A socket has no send_str primitive, so the text is copied into bytes here. A file
        # avoids that with fd_write_str, which hands write(2) the string's own fat pointer.
        # One allocation against one syscall is not worth a new generator yet.
        match self.send_all(data.to_bytes()):
            Result.Ok(_) -> return Result.Ok(~)
            Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e.to_io())

    ##:
    Writes bytes, and does not stop until every one has gone.

    - Parameter data: The bytes to write. The buffer stays the caller's.
    - Returns: Nothing.
    - Errors: IoError, when a write failed before the buffer was gone.
    :##
    fn write_bytes(poke self, u8[] data) ~ | IoError:
        match self.send_all(data):
            Result.Ok(_) -> return Result.Ok(~)
            Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e.to_io())

    ##:
    Does nothing, successfully.

    A socket is not buffered here: send_all() has already handed every byte to the kernel
    by the time it returns. The buffered writer is where the call earns its name.

    - Returns: Nothing.
    - Errors: IoError, never in practice.
    :##
    fn flush(poke self) ~ | IoError:
        return Result.Ok(~)

##:
Whether this handle still holds a descriptor.

- Returns: False once close() has run.
:##
extend TcpStream is_open() bool:
    return self.fd >= 0

##:
Whether this listener still holds a descriptor.

- Returns: False once close() has run.
:##
extend TcpListener is_open() bool:
    return self.fd >= 0

##:
Connects to a host and port.

The host may be a name or a numeric address; a name is resolved and every
answer is tried in turn. There is no connect timeout, so an address that
answers nothing waits for the kernel.

- Parameter host: The host to reach, as a name or an address.
- Parameter port: The port to connect to.
- Returns: The connected stream.
- Errors: NetError, when no answer for the host accepted a connection.
:##
public fn connect(string host, i32 port) TcpStream | NetError:
    let i32 fd = sock_tcp_connect(host, port)??
    return Result.Ok(TcpStream(fd))

##:
Binds a listening socket and starts accepting connections.

An empty host means the wildcard address. Port 0 asks the kernel to choose,
and .local_port() reads back what it chose. SO_REUSEADDR is always set, so a
port whose last connection is still closing binds again at once.

- Parameter host: The address to bind, or an empty string for the wildcard.
- Parameter port: The port to bind, or 0 to let the kernel choose.
- Parameter backlog: How many completed connections may wait to be accepted.
- Returns: The listening socket.
- Errors: NetError, when the address cannot be bound.
:##
public fn listen(string host, i32 port, i32 backlog) TcpListener | NetError:
    let i32 fd = sock_tcp_listen(host, port, backlog)??
    return Result.Ok(TcpListener(fd))

##:
Takes the next connection waiting on a listener, and the address of its peer.

The call blocks until one arrives, unless set_timeout() has given the
listener a bound, in which case it answers TimedOut. The address is the one
that accept(2) gives, so it costs no second system call. A dual-stack listener
gives an IPv4 peer as a V4-mapped V6 address.

- Returns: The accepted stream and the address of its peer.
- Errors: NetError, when no connection could be taken, or
  NetError.InvalidAddress when the peer is not an IPv4 or an IPv6 address.
- Example:
```sushi
use <collections/strings>

fn greet() ~ | NetError:
    let TcpListener listener = listen("127.0.0.1", 0, 8)??
    let TcpStream client = connect("127.0.0.1", listener.local_port()??)??
    let (TcpStream served, IpAddr peer) = listener.accept()??
    println("connection from {peer.text()}")
    served.send_all("Mostly Harmless".to_bytes())??
    let u8[] heard = client.recv_exact(15)??
    println(heard.to_string())
    return Result.Ok(~)

fn main() i32:
    match greet():
        Result.Ok(_) -> return 0
        Result.Err(_) -> return 1
```
:##
extend TcpListener accept() (TcpStream, IpAddr) | NetError:
    let (i32 fd, i32 version, u64 high, u64 low) = sock_tcp_accept(self.fd)??
    let TcpStream stream = TcpStream(fd)
    return Result.Ok((stream, peer_address(version, high, low)??))

##:
The IpAddr of a peer, from the address bits that accept(2) or getpeername(2) wrote.

- Parameter version: 4, 6, or 0 for an address family that is not IP.
- Parameter high: The high 64 bits of a V6 address; 0 for V4.
- Parameter low: The low 64 bits of a V6 address, or the V4 address.
- Returns: The address.
- Errors: NetError.InvalidAddress when the version is not 4 or 6.
:##
fn peer_address(i32 version, u64 high, u64 low) IpAddr | NetError:
    if (version == 4):
        return Result.Ok(IpAddr.V4(low as u32))
    if (version == 6):
        return Result.Ok(IpAddr.V6(high, low))
    return Result.Err(NetError.InvalidAddress)

##:
Opens a second handle over the same listening socket.

This is dup(2): an independent descriptor over the SAME open socket, so a connection
waiting on the port is taken by whichever handle calls accept() first. That is the
shared-listener pattern, where several workers accept on one port, and it is why a
listener gets share() while it implements no contract. Closing either handle leaves the
other accepting.

A listener has no .clone() (CE2431), because a copy verb would hide the second
descriptor. This is the operation that means one, and its name says so.

- Returns: A second owning listener over the same socket.
- Errors: IoError, when the descriptor could not be duplicated.
:##
extend TcpListener share() TcpListener | IoError:
    match sock_dup(self.fd):
        Result.Ok(fd) -> return Result.Ok(TcpListener(fd))
        Result.Err(e) -> return Result.Err(e.to_io())

##:
Writes bytes, and answers how many of them went.

One write may take fewer bytes than it was offered. A caller that needs the
whole buffer gone wants send_all() instead.

This is the one write that answers a COUNT: a socket's partial write says what the peer's
window took, and the contract's write_bytes(), which writes everything, cannot say that.
There is no recv() beside it. One read on a socket is the contract's read_bytes(), and
every NetError a read can answer has its IoError twin, so a second name would have
carried nothing of its own.

- Parameter data: The bytes to write. The buffer stays the caller's.
- Returns: How many bytes were written.
- Errors: NetError, when the write failed.
:##
extend TcpStream send(u8[] data) i32 | NetError:
    return Result.Ok(sock_send(self.fd, data)??)

##:
Writes every byte, looping until the buffer is gone.

- Parameter data: The bytes to write. The buffer stays the caller's.
- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: NetError, when a write failed before the buffer was gone.
:##
extend TcpStream send_all(u8[] data) ~ | NetError:
    let i32 total = data.len()
    let i32 done = 0
    while (done < total):
        let u8[] rest = data.ss(done, total - done)
        let i32 sent = sock_send(self.fd, rest)??
        if (sent <= 0):
            return Result.Err(NetError.Closed)
        done := done + sent
    return Result.Ok(~)

##:
Reads exactly `count` bytes, looping until they have all arrived.

A peer that closes early is an error, not a short answer: a caller asking for
an exact count has no use for a partial one.

- Parameter count: How many bytes to wait for.
- Returns: Exactly `count` bytes.
- Errors: NetError.Closed when the peer closed early, or the read's own error.
:##
extend TcpStream recv_exact(i32 count) u8[] | NetError:
    let u8[] out = from([])
    while (out.len() < count):
        let u8[] chunk = sock_recv(self.fd, count - out.len())??
        if (chunk.len() == 0):
            return Result.Err(NetError.Closed)
        out.extend(chunk)
    return Result.Ok(out)

##:
The address of the peer at the other end.

It reads the address bits that getpeername(2) wrote, as peer() does, and asks
no resolver. Call .text() on the answer for the canonical text.

- Returns: The peer's address.
- Errors: NetError, when the stream has no peer, or NetError.InvalidAddress
  when the peer is not an IPv4 or an IPv6 address.
:##
extend TcpStream peer_ip() IpAddr | NetError:
    let (IpAddr ip, _) = self.peer()??
    return Result.Ok(ip)

##:
The port of the peer at the other end.

- Returns: The peer's port.
- Errors: NetError, when the stream has no peer.
:##
extend TcpStream peer_port() i32 | NetError:
    return Result.Ok(sock_peer_port(self.fd)??)

##:
The address and the port of the peer at the other end, from one system call.

No resolver is asked, so this makes no network request.

- Returns: The peer's address and port.
- Errors: NetError, when the stream has no peer, or NetError.InvalidAddress
  when the peer is not an IPv4 or an IPv6 address.
- Example:
  ```sushi no_run
  let TcpStream s = connect("127.0.0.1", 8080).realise(TcpStream(-1))
  match s.peer():
      Result.Ok((ip, port)) -> println("{ip.text()}:{port}")
      Result.Err(_) -> println("no peer")
  ```
:##
extend TcpStream peer() (IpAddr, i32) | NetError:
    let (i32 version, u64 high, u64 low, i32 port) = sock_peer(self.fd)??
    return Result.Ok((peer_address(version, high, low)??, port))

##:
The port a listener actually bound.

This is the only way to learn the port after binding port 0.

- Returns: The bound port.
- Errors: NetError, when the socket is not bound.
:##
extend TcpListener local_port() i32 | NetError:
    return Result.Ok(sock_local_port(self.fd)??)

##:
The port a connected stream bound at this end.

- Returns: The local port.
- Errors: NetError, when the socket is not bound.
:##
extend TcpStream local_port() i32 | NetError:
    return Result.Ok(sock_local_port(self.fd)??)

##:
Bounds how long a read and a write on a stream may wait.

A bound that expires answers NetError.TimedOut. Without one a read waits for
as long as the peer stays silent.

- Parameter recv_ms: The read bound, in milliseconds.
- Parameter send_ms: The write bound, in milliseconds.
- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: NetError, when the option could not be set.
:##
extend TcpStream set_timeouts(i32 recv_ms, i32 send_ms) ~ | NetError:
    sock_set_recv_timeout(self.fd, recv_ms)??
    sock_set_send_timeout(self.fd, send_ms)??
    return Result.Ok(~)

##:
Bounds how long accept() may wait for a connection.

- Parameter accept_ms: The bound, in milliseconds.
- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: NetError, when the option could not be set.
:##
extend TcpListener set_timeout(i32 accept_ms) ~ | NetError:
    sock_set_recv_timeout(self.fd, accept_ms)??
    return Result.Ok(~)

##:
Closes a stream, and CONSUMES the handle.

The call takes the handle, so a read after a close is refused while compiling (CE2435)
rather than answering EBADF at run time, and there is nothing left for the scope exit to
close. One binding owns a socket, and this is where it stops owning it.

- Returns: Nothing. The handle is spent.
- Errors: NetError, when the descriptor could not be closed.
:##
extend TcpStream close(nom self) ~ | NetError:
    close_socket(poke self.fd)??
    return Result.Ok(~)

##:
Closes a listener, and CONSUMES the handle. The same terms as TcpStream.

- Returns: Nothing. The handle is spent.
- Errors: NetError, when the descriptor could not be closed.
:##
extend TcpListener close(nom self) ~ | NetError:
    close_socket(poke self.fd)??
    return Result.Ok(~)
